    'mole-excision': 'The clinician numbs the area and removes the mole with a surgical instrument. An excision wound may be closed with stitches, and tissue can be sent to a lab for examination. Before you leave, the team explains wound care and follow-up.',
    'punch-grafting': 'After numbing the areas involved, the surgeon uses a small circular instrument to take tiny grafts from healthy skin and places them in prepared spots in the area being treated. Both sites are dressed and checked as they heal.',
    'hair-analysis': 'The clinician examines the scalp and the pattern of shedding or thinning. They may check hair strength and examine the scalp under magnification. Blood tests or a scalp sample may be suggested if the cause is unclear.',
    'hair-transplant': 'With the scalp numbed, the team removes follicles from a donor area and places them into thinning areas. The donor method may involve individual follicular units or grafts prepared from a strip of scalp. The team explains care for both areas afterward.',
    'hair-reduction': 'For laser hair reduction, the clinician cleans the area and provides protective eyewear. A handheld device delivers pulses that target pigment in the hair. Follow-up sessions may be planned as different hairs enter the growth phase.',
    'prp-gfc': 'For PRP, the clinician draws a small blood sample, separates and concentrates the platelet-rich portion, then injects it into the planned scalp areas. GFC uses a different preparation, so the team should explain which system it uses and how it differs before treatment.',
    'artificial-hair-implantation': 'If synthetic fiber implantation is offered and suitable, the clinician discusses the implant material and places individual fibers into the scalp. Ask the clinic to confirm the device, its local regulatory status, infection and scarring risks, and how the fibers are maintained or removed.',
    'ultra-pulse-co2-fractional-laser': 'The clinician protects your eyes, prepares the treatment area and uses the laser to create microscopic columns of resurfacing with untreated skin between them. The visit ends with instructions for protecting the treated skin as it heals.',
    'cool-laser-therapy': 'The name can refer to different devices. At the assessment, the clinic should identify its device, explain how it contacts or passes over the skin, and confirm eye protection, cooling and aftercare for that specific treatment.',
    'laser-peel': 'After preparing the skin and providing eye protection, the clinician moves a laser handpiece across the selected area to resurface it. How much of the surface is treated and how it heals depend on the specific laser and plan.',
    'primelase-laser': 'For hair reduction, the clinician passes a cooled diode laser handpiece over the prepared area while you wear protective eyewear. The applicator delivers pulses to the selected area while cooling the skin at the point of contact.',
    'tribeam-laser': 'If a pigment-targeting laser session is appropriate, the clinician checks the marks first, provides protective eyewear and delivers brief pulses to selected areas. Confirm the TriBeam model and whether the clinician advises a test spot.',
    'revlite-laser': 'The clinician examines the area and provides eye protection. If RevLite is selected, the handpiece delivers short pulses to the planned treatment area. The clinician reviews your skin’s response and explains what to watch for afterward.',
    'laser-tattoo-removal': 'With eye protection in place, the clinician directs short laser pulses at selected ink colours. The body gradually clears some of the fragmented pigment. Sessions are spaced to let the skin heal and allow the clinician to assess remaining ink.',
    'sygmalift-hifu': 'After assessing the area, the clinician applies coupling gel and moves an ultrasound applicator over the planned treatment zones. Confirm the device and the areas being treated during your visit.',
    'secret-rf': 'After cleaning the skin and, when appropriate, using topical numbing, the clinician applies a sterile microneedle tip that delivers radiofrequency energy beneath the surface. Ask about the risk of burns, scarring and other complications before proceeding.',
    'sylfirm-x': 'The clinician prepares the skin and uses a sterile microneedle handpiece to deliver radiofrequency energy to the selected areas. Discussion before the session should cover the reason for choosing this device, possible pigment changes and other complications.',
    threadlift: 'After local numbing, the clinician places medical threads beneath the skin through small entry points and adjusts them to support the selected tissue. The team checks the area and explains what to expect as swelling settles.',
    botox: 'The clinician assesses facial movement, cleans the planned injection sites and uses a fine needle to inject botulinum toxin into selected muscles. At follow-up, the clinician can check the change in movement and appearance.',
    'skin-boosters': 'For injectable skin boosters, the clinician cleans the area and places small amounts of the chosen product into the planned sites. Product choice and treatment areas vary, so ask which formulation is proposed and what reactions to report.',
    'dermal-fillers': 'The clinician assesses contour, cleans the area and places the chosen filler using a needle or cannula. The clinician checks the area after placement and explains urgent symptoms to report. Accidental injection into a blood vessel is a rare but serious risk.',
    exosomes: 'There is no single standard procedure for cosmetic exosome use. Ask the clinic to identify the product, how it would be applied, whether it is authorized for that use locally, and what evidence supports its safety. Availability and suitability require clinical confirmation.',
    mesotherapy: 'Mesotherapy uses a series of shallow injections into the skin. The ingredients and evidence vary by purpose. Ask for the precise formulation, why it is being considered and whether other options have better evidence for your concern.',
    'hydra-facial': 'The clinician uses a handheld tip and solutions to cleanse and exfoliate the surface. Suction helps lift debris, and the session finishes by applying hydrating serum. The products used can vary with the chosen session.',
    'oxygeneo-facial': 'The clinician applies a primer gel and moves an exfoliating applicator over the skin. Depending on the chosen session, the clinician may follow with a serum application or additional device steps.',
    innopeel: 'Once the clinician confirms the InnoPeel formulation, they cleanse and prepare the skin, apply the selected peel and monitor the response. Removal and aftercare follow the instructions for that specific formulation.',
    'cosmelan-peel': 'A professional applies a pigment-targeting mask in clinic. The clinician tells you when to remove it and explains the prescribed home-care phase, including how they will monitor irritation and pigmentation.',
    'yellow-peel': 'The clinician prepares the skin, applies the selected formulation and checks the skin’s response. The product determines when and how the peel is removed and what to use afterward. Ask which formulation the clinic uses under this name.',
    'glycolic-acid-peel': 'After cleaning the skin, the clinician applies a glycolic acid solution and monitors the reaction. The formulation determines when and how it is removed or neutralized. The clinician then explains how to care for the skin as it recovers.'
